------------------------------------------------------------------------
Scenario 1: exact reported sequence (keyed decision, keyless blocked,
keyed resolved, task paused). Before the fix the drain listed the
keyless blocker on every wake.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

### reported sequence
$ cat state/task1.status
    needs-decision [key=dsh-search-cleanup-approval]: approve the cleanup
    blocked: dsh-search - w unreachable, remote delete paused
    resolved [key=dsh-search-cleanup-approval]: approved, executed
    paused: dsh-search cleanup complete
$ FM_STATE_OVERRIDE=state bin/fm-wake-drain.sh   (exit=0)
    (no output)
OPEN DECISIONS printed? no
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Scenario 2 (guard): the same keyless blocker with NO later terminal line
must still be listed - the rule is a supersede, not a blanket suppression.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

### keyless blocker, no terminal line
$ cat state/task1.status
    blocked: waiting on the forge
    working: polling the forge
$ FM_STATE_OVERRIDE=state bin/fm-wake-drain.sh   (exit=0)
    | OPEN DECISIONS (still open, folded from the durable status logs - not just the latest line):
    | task1 blocked: waiting on the forge
    | OPEN DECISIONS: close one by answering it: bin/fm-send.sh <task> --resolve-key <key> '<answer>'
OPEN DECISIONS printed? YES
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Scenario 3 (guard): a later unrelated terminal line must NOT clear a KEYED
open decision - keyed pairing/closing semantics unchanged.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

### keyed decision + unrelated done
$ cat state/task1.status
    needs-decision [key=api-shape]: pick REST or RPC
    done: unrelated later milestone
$ FM_STATE_OVERRIDE=state bin/fm-wake-drain.sh   (exit=0)
    | STATUS OUTCOME BACKSTOP (newest captain-facing task event has no covering branch outcome):
    | task1 done: unrelated later milestone
    | OPEN DECISIONS (still open, folded from the durable status logs - not just the latest line):
    | task1 [key=api-shape] needs-decision: pick REST or RPC
    | OPEN DECISIONS: close one by answering it: bin/fm-send.sh <task> --resolve-key <key> '<answer>'
OPEN DECISIONS printed? YES
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Scenario 4: keyless needs-decision (not only blocked) superseded by done,
and by failed - both terminal verbs shared with status_is_terminal_verb.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

### keyless needs-decision + done
$ cat state/task1.status
    needs-decision: choose a release target
    done: shipped the release
$ FM_STATE_OVERRIDE=state bin/fm-wake-drain.sh   (exit=0)
    | STATUS OUTCOME BACKSTOP (newest captain-facing task event has no covering branch outcome):
    | task1 done: shipped the release
OPEN DECISIONS printed? no

### keyless blocked + failed
$ cat state/task1.status
    blocked: waiting on the forge
    failed: the forge never came back
$ FM_STATE_OVERRIDE=state bin/fm-wake-drain.sh   (exit=0)
    | STATUS OUTCOME BACKSTOP (newest captain-facing task event has no covering branch outcome):
    | task1 failed: the forge never came back
OPEN DECISIONS printed? no
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Scenario 5 (adversarial): a fresh keyless decision raised AFTER a terminal
line is a NEW decision and must list again.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

### keyless reopen after a pause
$ cat state/task1.status
    blocked: first wait
    paused: first wait cleared
    blocked: the forge went down again
$ FM_STATE_OVERRIDE=state bin/fm-wake-drain.sh   (exit=0)
    | OPEN DECISIONS (still open, folded from the durable status logs - not just the latest line):
    | task1 blocked: the forge went down again
    | OPEN DECISIONS: close one by answering it: bin/fm-send.sh <task> --resolve-key <key> '<answer>'
OPEN DECISIONS printed? YES
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Scenario 6: span-scan consistency (the watcher/daemon path). The bounded
span scan must agree with the whole-file fold on the reported sequence.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

$ _fm span scan on reported.status (start offset 0)
    rc=1  actionable_record=''
    whole-file fold lists=''

$ _fm span scan on keyed.status (start offset 0)
    rc=0  actionable_record=needs-decision\ \[key=api-shape\]:\ pick\ REST\ or\ RPC\ \;\ done:\ unrelated\ later\ milestone
    whole-file fold lists=$'api-shape\tneeds-decision\tpick REST or RPC'

Expected: reported -> rc=1 and no fold record; keyed -> rc=0 naming the
api-shape decision while the keyless default record stays retired.
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Scenario 7: persisted span-scan cursor migration. A cursor written under
the previous scanner version (2) must be discarded and the span rebuilt,
not trusted with a stale live keyless origin.
------------------------------------------------------------------------
